{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—a quiet pink snow over ancient temples. No guidebook could prepare me for the hush of Kinkaku-ji, the golden pavilion shimmering in a still pond, or the rhythmic sweep of a monk’s broom in a Zen garden. I ate my first matcha parfait at a tucked-away café, the bitterness balanced by sweet red bean, and wandered Nishiki Market, where pickled radishes glowed like jewels and fresh tuna glistened under soft lights.\n\nTokyo was a symphony of neon and noise. In Shibuya, I stood in the crossing, swallowed by a river of people, yet felt oddly alone—each soul absorbed in their own world. I climbed to the observation deck of Tokyo Tower, watching dusk paint the city in violet and gold. That night, I slurped ramen in a basement stall, the steam fogging my glasses, the broth rich with umami magic.\n\nIn Hakone, I slept in a ryokan, wrapped in a yukata, feet bare on tatami. The onsen was a revelation—hot, mineral-rich water under a star-strewn sky, the scent of cedar and sulfur curling in the air. My host bowed deeply as she served breakfast: miso soup, grilled fish, pickled vegetables, and rice so perfectly cooked it felt like a prayer.\n\nJapan doesn’t shout. It whispers. A bow. A pause. A perfectly placed stone. I left with a quiet heart, carrying not souvenirs, but stillness. The train ride to Narita was silent except for the clack of wheels and the rustle of a man reading a newspaper. I didn’t need to speak. I’d been understood.\n\nI came for the temples, the food, the tech. I left with something deeper: the grace of simplicity, the beauty of restraint. Japan didn’t just welcome me—it changed me.\n\n(Word count: 298)", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.095